WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.770
Vsauce! Kevin here, and there’s a secret
message hidden in these 144 characters -- that’s

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:16.240
a 12x12 square grid and every card has its
own coordinates. A dozen dozen, a gross. I’m

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:23.220
gonna decode the message randomly using dice
to choose which cards I’ll turn over, and

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:29.830
I guarantee it will work perfectly in only
11 choices, no matter what they are -- then

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:38.990
I will show you how to do this with any message
of any length. Even an entire randomly-rolled

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:40.110
book.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:47.770
Here’s the setup. The grid has X and Y axes
numbered 1 through 12, so the very bottom

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:57.690
left card is at (1, 1) and the top right card
is at (12, 12). I have two 12-sided dice.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:03.190
One rolls for X, one rolls for Y. There’s
an equal probability of rolling everything

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:11.221
from a 1 to a 12. We can’t just use two
normal 6-sided dice to add up to 12, because

00:01:11.221 --> 00:01:16.830
we’d have a probability bell curve peaking
around 7 and it is literally impossible to

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:20.830
roll a 1 with 2 d6 dice.

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:30.440
Alright, here we go. Roll number one. Our
game begins. Okay, I got (3, 11), alright

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:37.140
so I’m gonna mark that card with a big X
and then I’ll remove all the cards above

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:44.620
it, below it, to the left, and to the right
-- we’re wiping out everything that shares

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:48.130
an X or a Y value with each roll.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:49.270
GOT IT?

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:59.641
Let’s continue Roll number two -- (7, 6).
Alright, (7,6) right here. Same thing, mark

00:01:59.641 --> 00:02:05.270
it with an X, wipe out the column and row.
I’m gonna keep playing, and if I roll a

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:12.170
number that corresponds to a missing coordinate,
like (5,6) which is now blank, I’ll just

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:15.640
re-roll until I get a hit.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:21.140
LISTEN. AT THIS POINT THERE’S SOMETHING
YOU NEED TO KNOW. DESPERATELY.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:27.420
I’m only rolling dice so that you know I’m
choosing cards randomly. But when you play

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:33.540
this with someone, you don’t need dice,
just ask the other person to choose whatever

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:39.510
cards they want. They’ll keep choosing until
there’s only one card left without an X,

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:45.700
and you’ll reveal the perfectly-decoded
message. And they’ll freak out. Since they’re

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:50.819
the ones choosing, they can be sure it’s
not rigged.

00:02:50.819 --> 00:03:13.760
More on that later -- for now let’s 80’s
action movie montage the rest of my rolls.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:55.260
Alright there’s only one blank card left
so that gets our final X. All of our selected

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:00.879
coordinates are listed here on the left. I’m
gonna put all of the cards in a row so that

00:04:00.879 --> 00:04:04.320
you can read them easily when I flip them
over.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:12.860
WAIT. How is this even possible? I put letters
on 144 cards, I randomly choose 12 of them,

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:20.349
and I’m left with exactly the 12 cards I
want in exactly the correct order? There’s

00:04:20.349 --> 00:04:26.930
no way this is gonna work -- RIGHT?

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:34.089
W...WR...WRON…. Exclamation point. WRONG!

00:04:34.089 --> 00:04:39.800
Because this is a variation on the Magic Square
discovered by Chinese mathematicians 2,200

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:46.080
years ago and tweaked a lot later by Canadian
postman and magician Stewart James. In a Magic

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:52.000
Square, adding up each row, column, and diagonal
gives you the same total, called the Magic

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.470
Constant. The number of rows and columns is
called the order. So this is a simple Order

00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:00.970
3 Magic Square with a Magic Constant of 15.

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:06.279
That’s not too hard for us now, it’s a
little like sodoku -- but it took another

00:05:06.279 --> 00:05:14.229
800 years for Varahamihira to decipher the
first 16-block Order 4 Magic Square in India.

00:05:14.229 --> 00:05:21.639
And 1,000 years after THAT, we knew there
are 880 distinctly different Magic Squares

00:05:21.639 --> 00:05:22.969
of Order 4.

00:05:22.969 --> 00:05:28.860
Now we have pandiagonal magic squares, multimagic
squares, methods for creating magic squares

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:35.590
of odd orders and even orders, and they’re
so complex that even Euler’s hypotheses

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:41.059
on constructing evenly odd ordered mutually
orthogonal Graeco-Latin squares turned out

00:05:41.059 --> 00:05:42.669
to be wrong.

00:05:42.669 --> 00:05:50.229
But the one magic square that has fascinated
for centuries, even outside the math community,

00:05:50.229 --> 00:05:56.379
is stuck in the background of an early-16th
century German engraving. Albrecht Dürer’s

00:05:56.379 --> 00:06:01.509
“Melancolia I” depicts a winged woman
who represents either melancholy and depression

00:06:01.509 --> 00:06:08.949
or geometry, depending on who you ask. She’s
surrounded by all sorts of hand tools, an

00:06:08.949 --> 00:06:16.181
hourglass, a compass, a dog... A dog is not
a hand tool. A dog is your best friend. And

00:06:16.181 --> 00:06:19.939
way in the back corner is a very unique magic
square.

00:06:19.939 --> 00:06:26.210
Dürer’s magic square isn’t technically
his -- it appears in Luca Pacioli’s “De

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:32.939
Viribus Quantitatis” about 15 years before
the engraving. Pacioli was Leonardo da Vinci’s

00:06:32.939 --> 00:06:37.979
teacher and best friend, and the odds that
Dürer coincidentally came up with the same

00:06:37.979 --> 00:06:43.680
one on his own are astronomical. The magic
constant in Dürer’s order 4 magic square

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:49.860
is 34 -- but the magic comes by more than
just adding rows, columns, and diagonals.

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:56.759
You can do each of the four quadrants to get
34. The 4 blocks in the center? 34. The outside

00:06:56.759 --> 00:06:59.690
4 corners? 34.

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:08.699
Inner rectangles, 34. Diagonal rhombuses,
34. The internal 3x3 blocks, 34.

00:07:08.699 --> 00:07:17.970
Vertical X? 34 and 34. Horizontal X? 34 and
34. Trapezoids, 34. Squares that don’t use

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:20.990
the corners? 34.

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:27.129
When you sort and stack just the right combination
of numbers, really incredible patterns happen

00:07:27.129 --> 00:07:33.189
-- and it’s the same with words. The classical
example is the SATOR or ROTAS square found

00:07:33.189 --> 00:07:39.169
in Pompeii and through Europe. The palindrome
TENET in the middle of the SATOR square? That’s

00:07:39.169 --> 00:07:44.259
where Christopher Nolan’s movie title came
from. Tenet. Puzzlemakers have successfully

00:07:44.259 --> 00:07:51.490
made magic word squares up to order 9, but
a true order 10 word square is still considered

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:54.559
the Holy Grail of recreational linguistics.

00:07:54.559 --> 00:08:01.679
I did a modified magic square with 12, and
I said you could do it even with a book of

00:08:01.679 --> 00:08:08.919
any length. Look, here’s the mind-blowingly
simple secret -- you just need to repeat your

00:08:08.919 --> 00:08:17.690
hidden message on as many rows as there are
characters in the word, message, or passage.

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:24.490
Hello is [H E L L O five times stacked]. You
can do Happy Birthday for someone. It'd just

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:30.990
be 14 rows including the space. You could
randomly re-create the entire Harry Potter

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:39.150
series, you’d just need… about a 5,327,000
x 5,327,000 grid of the book start to finish,

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:47.720
with a full book on each row, assuming the
series’ 1,084,170 words conform to an average

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:53.470
word length of 4.7 characters and there’s
a space in between each one. And how many

00:08:53.470 --> 00:08:59.940
choices would it take? If however many rows
or columns is equal to n, then it’ll take

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:04.850
n-1 choices, every single time.

00:09:04.850 --> 00:09:11.280
No matter how big your mystery grid is, every
time you make a selection and remove all the

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:17.500
other cards on that selection’s X and Y
planes, you eliminate possible duplicates

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:24.830
and isolate that choice as being the only
letter that can occupy that specific position

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:27.200
in the column or row.

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:35.700
It’s weirdly simple. It’s weirdly complex.
It’s elegant and amazing in a strange combination

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:39.550
of mathematical beauty and linguistics.

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:45.150
And you can grab a marker and some slips of
paper and play this secret message game to

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:52.660
reveal something monumental -- like proposing
to spend the rest of your life with your significant

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:53.890
other (MARRY ME?).

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:57.490
Or... get someone to unwittingly spell the
word “BUTT”

00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:00.820
And as always, thanks for watching.

